(>> Solution to Question 12:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the physical origin of Skin Effect and Proximity Effect in AC conductors, deriving formulas for skin depth.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Skin Effect, Proximity Effect, and AC Resistance:) Tj
T*
(   1. Skin Effect Physical Mechanism:) Tj
T*
(   - In a conductor carrying alternating current, the magnetic flux lines internal to the) Tj
T*
(   conductor link more intensely with inner concentric filaments than outer surface filaments.) Tj
T*
(   - The central core of the wire experiences higher back-EMF \(higher inductive reactance\), which) Tj
T*
(   opposes AC current flow in the center.) Tj
T*
(   - Consequently, AC current is forced outward toward the outer periphery \(skin\) of the) Tj
T*
(   conductor.) Tj
T*
(   2. Skin Depth \(Penetration Depth delta\):) Tj
T*
(   - Defined as the depth beneath the surface where current density drops to 1/e \(36.8%\) of its) Tj
T*
(   surface value:) Tj
T*
(   delta = sqrt\( 2 / \(omega * mu * sigma\) \) = sqrt\( 1 / \(pi * f * mu * sigma\) \).) Tj
T*
(   - For copper \(sigma = 5.8 * 10^7 S/m\) at f = 50 Hz, delta approx 9.3 mm; at f = 1 MHz, delta) Tj
T*
(   approx 0.066 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Consequence: Effective AC resistance R_ac is significantly higher than DC resistance R_dc:) Tj
T*
(   R_ac / R_dc > 1.) Tj
T*
(   3. Proximity Effect:) Tj
T*
(   - Arises when two or more AC conductors are positioned in close physical proximity.) Tj
T*
(   - The alternating magnetic field produced by current in one conductor distorts the current) Tj
T*
(   distribution in the adjacent conductor.) Tj
T*
(   - Currents flowing in opposite directions repel, concentrating current on outward sides;) Tj
T*
(   currents flowing in the same direction attract, concentrating current on inner faces.) Tj
T*
(   - Further increases effective resistance and power dissipation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
