BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 13:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Fortescue's Symmetrical Components Method, defining symmetrical operators and transformation matrices for unbalanced 3-phase analysis.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Symmetrical Components Method \(Fortescue's Theorem\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Principle:) Tj
T*
(   - Any set of three unbalanced, asymmetrical coplanar phasors \(Va, Vb, Vc\) can be uniquely) Tj
T*
(   decomposed into three symmetrical, balanced sets of components:) Tj
T*
(   \(a\) Positive Sequence \(Va1, Vb1, Vc1\): Equal magnitude, separated by 120 deg, same phase) Tj
T*
(   sequence as original system \(a-b-c\).) Tj
T*
(   \(b\) Negative Sequence \(Va2, Vb2, Vc2\): Equal magnitude, separated by 120 deg, reverse phase) Tj
T*
(   sequence \(a-c-b\).) Tj
T*
(   \(c\) Zero Sequence \(Va0, Vb0, Vc0\): Equal magnitude and strictly in phase with each other \(0 deg) Tj
T*
(   displacement\).) Tj
T*
(   2. The Symmetrical Operator 'a':) Tj
T*
(   - Complex unit vector imparting a 120 degree counterclockwise rotation:) Tj
T*
(   a = 1 /_ 120 deg = -1/2 + j*sqrt\(3\)/2 = exp\(j * 2*pi / 3\).) Tj
T*
(   a^2 = 1 /_ 240 deg = -1/2 - j*sqrt\(3\)/2.) Tj
T*
(   a^3 = 1 /_ 360 deg = 1.) Tj
T*
(   Key identity: 1 + a + a^2 = 0.) Tj
T*
(   3. Synthesis Equations:) Tj
T*
ET

