(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Unbalanced 3-Phase Star Load with Isolated Neutral, and derive the neutral shift voltage using Millman's Theorem.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Unbalanced Star Load with Isolated Neutral \(Neutral Shift\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Problem Nature:) Tj
T*
(   - An unbalanced star load consisting of unequal phase admittances Y_R, Y_Y, Y_B is connected to) Tj
T*
(   a balanced 3-phase supply \(voltages V_RN, V_YN, V_BN relative to supply neutral N\).) Tj
T*
(   - The load neutral point 'n' is isolated \(floating, no neutral wire\).) Tj
T*
(   - Because impedances are unequal, the load neutral 'n' shifts away from supply neutral 'N',) Tj
T*
(   establishing a non-zero Neutral Displacement Voltage V_nN.) Tj
T*
(   2. Derivation of V_nN via Millman's Theorem:) Tj
T*
(   - Applying KCL at isolated load neutral 'n':) Tj
T*
(   I_Rn + I_Yn + I_Bn = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Express currents in terms of load branch voltages:) Tj
T*
(   \(V_R - V_n\) * Y_R + \(V_Y - V_n\) * Y_Y + \(V_B - V_n\) * Y_B = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Expand terms:) Tj
T*
(   V_R * Y_R + V_Y * Y_Y + V_B * Y_B = V_n * \(Y_R + Y_Y + Y_B\).) Tj
T*
(   - Since supply neutral N is 0 V \(V_R = V_RN, etc.\):) Tj
T*
(   V_nN = [ V_RN * Y_R + V_YN * Y_Y + V_BN * Y_B ] / [ Y_R + Y_Y + Y_B ].) Tj
T*
(   3. Physical Hazard:) Tj
T*
(   - Once V_nN is non-zero, the voltages across the individual load phases become unequal:) Tj
T*
(   V_load_R = V_RN - V_nN.) Tj
T*
(   - A phase with high impedance can experience severe overvoltage, destroying connected) Tj
T*
(   equipment.) Tj
T*
