12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze Hay's Bridge and explain why it is uniquely suited for measuring High-Q inductors.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Hay's Bridge for High-Q Inductor Measurement:) Tj
T*
(   1. Motivation:) Tj
T*
(   - In Maxwell's bridge, measuring high-Q coils \(Q > 10\) requires an impractically large parallel) Tj
T*
(   resistor R1.) Tj
T*
(   - Hay's bridge modifies Arm 1 by placing capacitor C1 in SERIES with resistor R1.) Tj
T*
(   2. Bridge Balance Equation:) Tj
T*
(   - Arm 1: Z1 = R1 - j / \(omega * C1\).) Tj
T*
(   - Arm 4: Zx = Rx + j * omega * Lx.) Tj
T*
(   - General balance condition: Zx * R1_arm = R2 * R3:) Tj
T*
(   \(Rx + j * omega * Lx\) * [ R1 - j / \(omega * C1\) ] = R2 * R3.) Tj
T*
(   - Expanding product:) Tj
T*
(   Rx * R1 + \(Lx / C1\) + j * [ omega * Lx * R1 - Rx / \(omega * C1\) ] = R2 * R3.) Tj
T*
(   3. Solving for Rx and Lx:) Tj
T*
(   - Equating real and imaginary parts:) Tj
T*
(   Rx = \(omega^2 * C1^2 * R1 * R2 * R3\) / \(1 + omega^2 * C1^2 * R1^2\).) Tj
T*
